{"key":"t65-bio-2.5","kind":"mcq","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"C","final_answer":"Active transport, because glucose moves from lower to higher concentration, which requires an energy input.","working":"The cell is taking up glucose even though the glucose concentration inside the cell is already higher than in the lumen. This means net glucose movement is against its concentration gradient. Passive processes such as simple diffusion and facilitated diffusion move solutes down their concentration gradient, so they cannot explain this uptake. Osmosis refers to water movement, not glucose transport. Moving glucose from lower to higher concentration requires energy, either directly or indirectly. In intestinal epithelial cells, this occurs by secondary active transport via the sodium-glucose symporter, powered by the Na+ gradient.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":465,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":209,"cacheReadTokens":256},"outputTokens":632,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":156,"reasoningTokens":476},"totalTokens":1097,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":465,"completion_tokens":632,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":256,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":209,"total_tokens":1097,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":256},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":476}},"reasoningTokens":476,"cachedInputTokens":256},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":8864,"keyed_label":"C"}
