{"key":"t65-bio-x4","kind":"mcq","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"A","final_answer":"Much of the heat added to water is used to break hydrogen bonds between water molecules before the molecules can move faster.","working":"Water's high specific heat is largely due to hydrogen bonding. When heat is added, much of the energy is used to break or weaken hydrogen bonds between water molecules rather than increasing molecular kinetic energy, so temperature rises slowly. Covalent bonds within water molecules do not need to break, water is polar rather than nonpolar, and density alone does not trap heat in this way.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":465,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":81,"cacheReadTokens":384},"outputTokens":241,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":127,"reasoningTokens":114},"totalTokens":706,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":465,"completion_tokens":241,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":384,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":81,"total_tokens":706,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":384},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":114}},"reasoningTokens":114,"cachedInputTokens":384},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":3864,"keyed_label":"A"}
{"key":"t65-bio-x3","kind":"mcq","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"B","final_answer":"The frequency of alleles for dark color increases in the population.","working":"This scenario describes natural selection (directional selection). Light-colored beetles are more easily spotted and eaten by predatory birds on the darkened bark, resulting in lower survival and reproductive success compared to dark-colored beetles. Because coloration is heritable, dark-colored beetles pass their alleles to subsequent generations at a higher rate. Over many generations, the frequency of alleles conferring dark coloration will increase in the population. Choice A incorrectly invokes Lamarckian inheritance of acquired traits. Choice C is incorrect because differential survival directly affects reproductive output. Choice D is incorrect because evolution occurs in populations over generations, not within an individual or single generation in this manner.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":413,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":413,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":359,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":171,"reasoningTokens":188},"totalTokens":772,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":188,"promptTokenCount":413,"candidatesTokenCount":171,"totalTokenCount":772,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":413}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":171}]},"reasoningTokens":188,"cachedInputTokens":0},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":7696,"keyed_label":"B"}
