{"key":"t65-bio-x4","kind":"mcq","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"A","final_answer":"Much of the heat added to water is used to break hydrogen bonds between water molecules before the molecules can move faster.","working":"Specific heat is the amount of heat that must be absorbed or lost for 1 g of a substance to change its temperature by 1°C. Temperature measures the average kinetic energy (motion) of molecules. In liquid water, extensive hydrogen bonding exists between molecules. When heat is added, a significant portion of the thermal energy is initially absorbed to disrupt these hydrogen bonds rather than immediately increasing the kinetic energy (speed) of the molecules. Consequently, water requires more heat to increase in temperature than substances lacking extensive hydrogen bonding.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":412,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":412,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":354,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":160,"reasoningTokens":194},"totalTokens":766,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":194,"promptTokenCount":412,"candidatesTokenCount":160,"totalTokenCount":766,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":412}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":160}]},"reasoningTokens":194,"cachedInputTokens":0},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":4320,"keyed_label":"A"}
